**Reccomendations

* Create a dedicated Movement to Work website that provides more detailed programme information
* Keep the strong visual hierarchy and simplicity used within the existing poster

* Create a clear hero section that immediately explains:
  * What Movement to Work is
  * That it is a two-week work-experience programme
  * The approximate programme hours
  * The main benefits of taking part

* Use strong calls to action such as "Explore the Programme" and "Speak to Your Work Coach"
* Include a visual two-week programme timeline showing what happens during each day
* Create a dedicated skills section explaining what participants can develop
* Clearly explain transferable skills and how they can be used within job applications
* Include information about CV creation and improvement
* Add interview preparation and mock-interview guidance
* Include support for cover letters, personal statements and job applications
* Provide guidance on appropriate AI use for applications and interview preparation
* Add information about local employers and employment sectors
* Include travel information alongside relevant opportunities
* Add an FAQ section answering common concerns before joining
* Clearly communicate the certificate of participation and how it can strengthen a CV
* Add a "What Happens Next?" section showing possible routes after completing the programme
* Use interactive elements such as quizzes to improve engagement
* Consider a skills or career quiz to help users identify their strengths
* Keep navigation simple and predictable
* Use short sections rather than large blocks of text
* Design mobile-first because some users may primarily access the website through a phone
* Ensure the website meets accessibility and colour-contrast requirements
* Use relevant workplace imagery to make the programme feel more approachable
* Allow users to access important information independently without relying entirely on a Work Coach
